# syn -- the shell grammar that belongs to a command's PAGE, not to your answer.
#
# the panel's `shell syntax` section is built from the line you typed: it
# explains the operators that answer uses. that is right for an operator, and
# wrong for a pattern. `ls` is the command whose arguments the shell expands
# before ls is ever started, and which half of level 2 is about -- so the page
# for ls showed [...] and * on the one card whose answer happened to use them
# and nothing on the twenty before it. a reference you have to re-open on a
# different card to see the rest of is not a reference.
#
# so: the rows listed here are shown on that command's page ALWAYS, merged
# with the ones the answer earns and de-duplicated, in table order either way.
#
# what earns a line: the corpus drills this command WITH a pattern, so the
# pattern is part of how the command is used rather than a thing that happened
# to be in one answer. that is the five below -- the three whose arguments the
# shell globs, and the two whose arguments are regexes (the table rows say
# both meanings, which is the whole reason they are one row and not two).
# a pipe earns no line anywhere: it sits between two commands, and the answer
# that uses one says so on its own.
#
# format: NAME<TAB>label<TAB>label... -- the labels are learn/syntax's own,
# spelled exactly, and selftest fails on one that is not in that table or on a
# NAME with no ref page. three is the ceiling: every row here is a row the
# panel spends on every card that names the command, out of the same budget
# the flags come from.
ls	*	?	[...]
find	*	?	[...]
grep	*	?	[...]
sed	*	[...]
awk	*	[...]
